---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout title="Retouching: Frequency Separation and Liquify" description="Separate tone from texture, retouch each independently, then reshape raster content with a non-destructive, re-editable liquify field. Local to your machine.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Retouching
      </nav>

      <h1>Retouching: Frequency Separation and Liquify</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        Two raster workflows that stay honest about what they are: an exact two-band
        tone/texture split, and a brush-driven deformation stored as editable state
        rather than baked pixels.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Frequency Separation</h2>
        <p>
          Split a raster layer into a tone/colour layer and a texture layer, retouch each
          independently, and let Varve recombine them. The split is exact by construction
          for flat content and within one 8-bit step per channel elsewhere — the dialog
          shows the measured reconstruction error before you commit.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Radius is Gaussian sigma in layer pixels: zoom, preview scale, and export scale never change it.</li>
          <li>Tone and texture are ordinary layers, so clone, heal, paint, masks, and layer effects all work on either band.</li>
          <li>Change the radius later and Varve re-splits the current retouched state — your edits are not discarded.</li>
          <li>Flatten bakes the recombined result into one layer whenever you want to move on.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/retouching')}>Read the step-by-step retouching guide →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Liquify</h2>
        <p>
          Push, expand, contract, twirl, restore, and smooth raster content with a brush.
          The deformation is stored as a bounded displacement field on the layer, so every
          stroke stays re-editable, reversible, and exportable without accumulating
          resampling blur.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Freeze and thaw protection is available on raster targets; shared separation deformation keeps its unsupported group-level freeze state explicit.</li>
          <li>Pressure controls size and strength; push follows the pointer without drifting when you hold still.</li>
          <li>Deform one component of a frequency separation, or the recombined composite so tone and texture stay aligned.</li>
          <li>Reset, undo, and re-entry recover committed deformation instead of restarting from a flattened screenshot.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Built for real texture, not just portraits</h2>
        <p>
          The same tools clean product shots, even out fabric and material texture, rescue
          scanned artwork and paint surfaces, and correct local tone. There is no face
          detection, no automatic smoothing, and no universal "correct" radius: you judge
          the texture scale on your own content.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section note">
        <h2>No hidden AI, no hidden cost</h2>
        <p>
          Both workflows run entirely on your machine with no model download, cloud
          inference, or network access. They operate in Varve's 8-bit working
          representation; the precision contract is stated above and measured in the app
          rather than implied. Freeze protection on a shared separation deformation is not
          available in this version, and geometry-level vector liquify is a separate future
          capability — the interface says so rather than silently rasterizing your vectors or text.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <h2>Shape the pixels without giving them up</h2>
        <a href={sitePath('/download')} class="btn-default">Download Varve</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .feature-page h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.5rem); font-family: var(--font-editorial); margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-intro { font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-section p, .feature-section li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; }
  .feature-section li + li { margin-top: 0.65rem; }
  .note { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary); padding-left: 1rem; }
  .feature-cta { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding-top: 2rem; }
  .feature-cta h2 { color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
</style>
